title op3: two directories merging into one

# Remark 16.6: for directories, names matter and identity does not,
# so two directories fully moving into one is a merge and not a
# conflict.  Both beliefs stand.  Nothing has to decide which side
# "is" the target, because a directory cannot be hard-linked and
# there is nothing about it a user can observe except its name.
#
# Off-of empties /a and /c into a rebuilt /d.  The two files keep
# their dnodes -- they were moved, not copied -- and the directory
# does not.  Onto is quiet.
#
# Phase 1: p's red edge lands under /d, nominating (base /a,
# off-of /d); q's does the same for (base /c, off-of /d).  The
# sibling generator finds both pairs as well, since /a and /c are
# deletions and /d an addition under the green root.
#
# Phase 2: /a's only voter votes into /d, and /d is /a's unique
# target, so /a moved.  Independently, /c's only voter votes into /d,
# so /c moved too.  Belief requires a unique TARGET and deliberately
# does not require a unique CLAIMANT, which is exactly what lets both
# stand -- and the reason it can afford to is Remark 16.6.  Under a
# unique-claimant rule this would be an ambiguity to report, and the
# report would be about a distinction no user can see.
#
# OPEN, and this fixture is the probe.  Which of onto's two directory
# dnodes the merged output reuses is emission freedom pinned only for
# determinism: successor question S3, "manifest order for now."  The
# gold below takes the lower-sorted base name, so /a's dnode 2
# survives and /c's dnode 4 does not.  An engine that picks 4 has
# implemented the other pin.  That is a finding about S3, not a
# defect, and this fixture is what would settle it.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: a/
      3-100: p = pv0
    4-100: c/
      5-100: q = qv0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: a/
      3-100: p = pv0
    4-100: c/
      5-100: q = qv0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    10-300: d/
      3-100: p = pv0
      5-100: q = qv0

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: d
      3-100: p = pv0
      5-100: q = qv0
